statement:
You get two strings `s` and `t` of the same length made of lowercase letters, plus two arrays `nextCost` and `previousCost` of 26 integers each. Picture the alphabet as a circle and number its letters from 0 (`'a'`) to 25 (`'z'`). As often as you like, you may take any one character of `s` and move it a single step around that circle:

- a step forward (`'z'` wraps around to `'a'`) costs `nextCost[j]`;
- a step backward (`'a'` wraps around to `'z'`) costs `previousCost[j]`;

where `j` is the number of the letter the character holds just before that step. Return the minimum total cost of steps that makes `s` equal to `t`.

validator:
function validate([s, t, nextCost, previousCost]) {
  if (s.length < 1 || s.length > 100000) return 's needs 1 to 10^5 letters.';
  if (t.length !== s.length) return 's and t must have the same length.';
  if (!/^[a-z]+$/.test(s) || !/^[a-z]+$/.test(t)) return 's and t use only lowercase letters.';
  if (nextCost.length !== 26 || previousCost.length !== 26) return 'nextCost and previousCost need exactly 26 values each.';
  if (nextCost.some(v => v < 0 || v > 1e9) || previousCost.some(v => v < 0 || v > 1e9)) return 'Each cost is between 0 and 10^9.';
  return null;
}

